                            <a name="comment2887"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: So Long, Mr. Whipple
</h4>

<p>
Hank, Hank, Hank,<br />
<br />
I just adore your writing style. Love how you can yank things I see on television or on my grocery store aisle and just plug them effortlessly into a story like an extra ply that I didn't know I needed until you rubbed my face in it. <br />
<br />
I was actually complaining the other day about how expensive TP is now unless you buy that no-frills single roll. Everything else (with bigger cardboard inserts) costs a fortune and you constantly change the roll unless you get the gargantuan (I love that word. I so rarely get to use it in a sentence - Elle from Kill Bill Vol. 2) double roll. <br />
<br />
Anyway, Mr. Whipple is infused into my soul as is Madge and the Maytag repairman.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:wolff@commercialappeal.com">Cindy</a>
	

 at November 23,2007 20:41
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2888"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: So Long, Mr. Whipple
</h4>

<p>
"So long, Mr. Whipple, master of reverse psychology. Thanks to you, we squeezed it like nobody's business."<br />
<br />
What a walk-away. I've been reading it in the Bud Light "Real Men of Genius" voice, but I can't get through it without laughing and breaking into, "Ahhh...Mr. Whipple."<br />
<br />
I wonder how many folks read this story in the paper while dropping the kids off at the pool. I can almost hear my dad chuckling from behind the bathroom door.
</p>
